{"kind":"assert","text":"Revised assessment of the previous-snapshot availability condition of add29762 (#20008), replacing #20061 (a6824ac8) per the requester's disposition c4d5dce8 (#20070) and review ee70880b. Sources at main 1fba3d55 unless stated.\n\nWhere a previous snapshot can exist. (1) In the resident process: [snapshotWithSource](internal/app/app.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:2490) keeps snapshotCache, snapshotFolder, snapshotProfile and snapshotSource under snapshotMu. On a same-profile head miss it keeps that cache while it loads the tail; the ordinary forward case folds the new events onto the retained folder and publishes the new snapshot, and the cold case (no reader, discarded reader, or a tail that does not start at the cache head) replaces the reader and re-audits with the old cache still in memory until the new one is published. A profile change is compared first (snapshotProfile != profile) and takes the cold path; the old projection is never re-published under a new profile. (2) In the browser: [useWorkroom](ui/src/lib/store.ts@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:24) keeps the last Status it received and shows it until the next status or wait returns; on failure it marks the page offline and keeps the Status. (3) Nowhere after a resident restart: the cache is process memory, and what a restart verifies is the signed checkpoint, which is the fresh answer, not a previous one.\n\nWho consumes a status and what they check. CLI: [validateRemoteFrontier](cmd/gs/main.go) compares the resident's frontier to the local ref and falls back to a local verified read on mismatch. MCP: [currentResidentOrientation](cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:1479) refuses an orientation whose frontier is not the local head read before and after the call. Browser and direct HTTP: [api.status and api.wait](ui/src/lib/api.ts@1fba3d550091f720e48521bb89f222bd4c218af8:408) apply whatever the resident returns with no local comparison; the browser has no local ref to compare against. #20061 wrongly generalised the CLI and MCP checks to every consumer; the browser is the consumer for which this condition matters.\n\nCases, with the governing restriction for each rejected one.\n\nA. An already-open browser during normal forward catch-up. The wait blocks briefly while the incremental tail is folded; the page keeps showing the status it last received. That status was current and verified when sent. Duration is the incremental fold, sub-second at these depths; no qualification is owed beyond what the wait contract already gives (the next answer carries the new frontier). Lawful and unchanged.\n\nB. An already-open browser during a cold re-audit or a long verification. The wait blocks for the whole audit (statusFromLive joins the single flight); the page keeps showing its retained status with nothing on screen saying the resident is rebuilding, until an HTTP failure marks it offline. That is the one case where a previously verified, still interpretable status is displayed during a rebuild, and it is lawful: the resident serves nothing old (the status endpoint still waits for the flight), the browser re-displays what it was already given, and no act can be filed against it, since every act goes to the resident and is judged at sequencing against the current log. What was missing is the qualification the condition asks for. Met at head 740672718b71bef8da5db651dfbcdc9dfd3d50af: while a wait is outstanding for more than a second, the page probes the non-blocking /v0/rebuild endpoint and, when the resident reports a rebuild running, shows on every view \"Showing frontier \u003chead\u003e at depth N from before the resident began verifying durable history; \u003cverified\u003e of \u003ctotal\u003e records verified. Nothing here is current until that finishes.\" (ui/src/lib/rebuild.ts, ui/src/lib/store.ts, ui/src/components/TopBar.tsx). The notice disappears when the wait returns. This adds no server surface and changes no contract; it is the browser saying truthfully what it is showing.\n\nC. A new reader (browser with no retained status, CLI, MCP) during a rebuild. The resident has, at most, the in-process same-profile cache of case B. Serving it would require the status endpoint to answer with a frontier that is not the head of the ref. The governing restrictions: the resident status contract anchors every answer to one exact frontier the caller can compare (service.Status.Cursor.Frontier, and DurableChanged treats any frontier difference as change); the MCP adapter would refuse it (frontier != local head) and the CLI would discard it, so a qualified answer would have to be a new field or endpoint that those clients learn to accept; and admission would still have to refuse acts judged against it. That is a new governed interpretation of what a status is, not a mechanical exposure, and this request says such a choice is adopted before it is implemented. The browser today shows the existing rebuild notice with progress (ui/src/components/RebuildNotice.tsx), which is honest. Not implemented here; a bounded proposal for a separate read-only previous-frontier surface is filed alongside this assessment for ordinary adoption, and nothing in this delivery depends on it.\n\nD. Restart without a cache. Nothing previous exists in the process; the checkpoint is what the rebuild verifies. Rejected because there is nothing to expose; the rebuild notice and progress are the whole truthful answer.\n\nE. Profile invalidation. snapshotWithSource discards the cache when the profile differs, and the rebuild notice says why a profile-invalid checkpoint cannot be shown. The request itself forbids exposing a projection invalidated by a profile change; the old cache describes a contract the binary no longer implements. Rejected by the request's own condition and by the existing contract; unchanged.\n\nF. Rewind. A rewound ref yields \"refuse verified frontier rollback\" from the kernel (headwatch_test.go, rewind case): no snapshot is published and the old cache describes a frontier no longer on the ref. Serving it as anything but history would misdescribe the ref; the browser's retained status in this case carries the case-B notice until the wait returns, and the wait returns the refusal as an error, which marks the page offline. Rejected for serving; qualified for retention.\n\nG. Unverifiable history. Verification fails; nothing is published; there is no interpretable previous snapshot to speak for. Rejected; the failure is the answer.\n\nProjection ownership. The retained snapshotFolder is mutated only under snapshotMu by the incremental path; a frozen copy for a separate read surface would have to be taken at publication time (the *snapshotCache value is already a copy of the header, but Projection shares slices with the folder), so case C would also need an immutable copy discipline before it could be safe. That is a further reason it belongs in a proposal, not in this head.\n\nDisposition requested. Condition 2 is met for the legal observational case (B) at head 740672718b71bef8da5db651dfbcdc9dfd3d50af and rejected with a named restriction for C through G; C is carried by a proposal for ordinary adoption. Planner: please record the requester's disposition against add29762 on this basis or say what is still missing.","body":{"condition":"previous-snapshot availability (revised)","head":"740672718b71bef8da5db651dfbcdc9dfd3d50af","replaces":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a6824ac8fc4bb245adba9392e5d0b5301464d1a7","request":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:add297623a7c5f1d22725d891def3bcfe13ab87a","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2d521563cf60b09d287f04e71667ceae931d4356, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b5e522348845f5ffd08d6a15b2e6cab4f49af377 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0